# What NOT to ship in the VSIX.
#
# This used to be an allowlist: a long list of `!server/node_modules/...`
# re-includes, because the bundled language server was plain `tsc` output that
# `require`d its dependencies by name at runtime, so its whole production closure
# had to be packaged. Two shipped bugs came from that list going stale.
#
# There is no bundled server any more. The extension is one esbuild bundle
# (`client/out/extension.js`) plus the manifest and the icon, and the language
# server comes from the user's own project. So this is a plain exclude list, and
# nothing here needs updating when a dependency changes.

# Sources and build inputs: the bundle is the artifact.
**/*.ts
**/*.map
**/tsconfig.json
**/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
client/src/**
scripts/**

# `tsc -b` and esbuild both write into client/out: tsc emits one .js per source
# file (typechecking and the test build need it), esbuild writes the single
# bundle the manifest points at. Only the bundle belongs in the VSIX. Without
# this the archive also carried a .js for every module -- compiled *tests*
# included -- and, because tsc does not clean, stale ones for source files that
# had since been deleted.
client/out/**
!client/out/extension.js

# Test and tooling config.
**/jest.config.js
**/babel.config.js
**/.babelrc.js
eslint.config.mjs
**/package-lock.json

# Resolution fixtures: real npm/pnpm/yarn installs, for CI only.
fixtures/**

# node_modules is not needed at all: everything the extension runs is bundled.
node_modules/**
client/node_modules/**

# Belt and braces. `server/` is deleted, but a stale local copy -- untracked
# build output and its node_modules from before the deletion -- is still on the
# disk of anyone who had the old checkout, and `vsce package` packages the
# working tree rather than the index. Without this line such a checkout produces
# a VSIX that ships a second implementation of Val. Verified: it did.
server/**

.github/**
.vscode/**
.gitignore
